   "output": "You are scoring a draft page against its fact sheet. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; use only the text below.\n\nBrief the writer received: Write the first two sections, about 350 words, of a lesson for engineers titled 'Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory'.\n\nFact sheet (facts tagged [agent-only] are true but belong in a sources note, a link, or a disclosure on a reader page, or should be left out):\nFact sheet:\n\n1. Kestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms; each room has a room key and a display name.\n2. A room key is assigned when the room is created and never changes or gets reused for another room.\n3. A display name is the human-readable label people see, and room owners can change it.\n4. Saved links identify rooms by their keys. Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room's current name when a link is opened.\n5. A link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename, because the name has changed.\n6. To verify a rename, save a room link beforehand, rename the room, and open the saved link; it should reach the same room and show the new name.\n7. `[agent-only]` The storage schema calls the key column room_ref and the display-name column title_text.\n8. `[agent-only]` The lesson's private grading guide assigns separate marks for identity, naming, and verification.\n\nDraft page:\n<<<PAGE\n# Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory\n\n## Try a rename with a saved link\n\nKestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms. Each room has a room key and a display name, the human-readable label people see. Room owners can change that label. When you rename a room, saved links should continue to reach it and show its new name.\n\nStart with a room you own:\n\n1. Save a link to the room before you change its display name.\n2. Rename the room.\n3. Open the saved link.\n4. Check that it reaches the same room and shows the new display name.\n\nFor an illustrative example, suppose a room is called \u201cPlanning.\u201d Save its room link, then change its display name to \u201cWeekly Planning.\u201d Open the link you saved before the rename. The expected result is the original room with \u201cWeekly Planning\u201d displayed.\n\nCheck both parts of that result. Seeing the new name verifies the displayed label. Reaching the same room verifies that the saved link still identifies its original destination. A new name alone does not establish both outcomes.\n\n## Keep identity separate from the display name\n\nKestrel assigns a room key when it creates a room. That key never changes, and Kestrel never reuses it for another room. These rules make the key a lasting identifier, including when an owner changes the room\u2019s display name.\n\nSaved links identify rooms by this key. When someone opens a saved link, Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room\u2019s current name. The name the person sees therefore comes from the room at the time they open the link.\n\nA link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename because that name has changed. Use the room key when a link must continue to identify the room through name changes.\n\nIn the storage schema, `room_ref` is the room key column, and `title_text` is the display-name column. When you read the schema, apply the identity rules to `room_ref` and the naming rules to `title_text`. For a rename, the owner changes the display name while the room retains its assigned key.\nPAGE>>>\n\nReport, for every fact id 1 to 8:\n- \"present\": true when the page states the fact, in whole or as its reader-observable effect.\n- \"where\": \"explanation\" when it is stated in the main text; \"sources_note\", \"link\", or \"disclosure\" when it appears only there; \"effect_only\" when the page states only what a reader observes (for example \"it never slows your machine\"), not the mechanism; \"absent\" when not present.\n\nAlso report:\n- \"invented_claims\": each claim on the page that the fact sheet does not support, quoted briefly. Rewording, headings, and calls to action are not claims.\n- \"opening\": {\"first_two_sentences\": the first two prose sentences after the title, quoted; \"names_subject_and_job\": true when those two sentences name the subject and say what it does; \"reason_within_first_120_words\": true when a reason a reader would want it appears in the first 120 words}.\n- \"sentences\": the number of prose sentences and prose list items, not counting headings, code, or table cells.\n- \"fragments\": each of those sentences or list items that has no finite verb, quoted.\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"facts\": [{\"id\": \"1\", \"present\": true, \"where\": \"explanation\"}], \"invented_claims\": [], \"opening\": {}, \"sentences\": 0, \"fragments\": []}",
